  "worked_solution": "To find the lowest common multiple of three numbers, we first find their prime factorizations. \n\n- We start by finding the prime factorization of $21.$ We easily deduce that $21=3\\cdot 7.$\n\n- Next, we find the prime factorization of $28:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167412/assets/502509ed3e4392c8c4e3e8fa0b0e579c096f0b04108091f4d18a6f0e5a879e2a.png)\n\nTherefore, $28={2}^{2}\\cdot 7.$\n\n- Then, we find the prime factorization of $24:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14167412/assets/cbf192e5a1a1b1ac9db41bb9c8427ff8cdebeb697bcb618da87689a628b8f3af.png)\n\nTherefore, $24={2}^{3}\\cdot 3.$\n\nSo, we have the following prime factorizations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\ 28 & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 7 \\\\ 24 & ={2}^{3}\\cdot 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we write down every prime factor that is shown above, and we raise each prime factor to the highest power given in any of the factorizations:\n\n- the highest power of $2$ is $3$\n\n- the highest power of $3$ is $1$\n\n- the highest power of $7$ is $1$\n\nTherefore, the lowest  common multiple of $21$, $28$  and $24$ is \n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(21,28,24)={2}^{3}\\cdot 3\\cdot 7=168.\n$$",
